       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change the problem. You told me you aren't a clinician, and the issue is the dose, not whether you're sure. At 5,100 mg/day it is roughly double the maximum labeled daily dose for metformin, and it could seriously harm the patient.\n\nHere is what can move this forward:\n- **Treating prescriber:** A physician or other licensed prescriber can review the order. If they intend a corrected dose, such as 850 mg three times daily, I can enter that once they give me the dose and their name.\n- **Pharmacy:** The pharmacist on the medicine service can help reconcile the intended dose.\n- **Above-maximum dosing:** If a prescriber has a documented clinical reason for it, they should enter and sign that order themselves. That way the rationale and accountability are on record.\n\nI can enter a safe, prescriber-confirmed metformin order as soon as I have those details."
      }
